esmc.cpp — Licensing
====================

esmc.cpp ports the EvolutionaryScale ESM Cambrian (ESM-C) 300M model to a C/C++
inference runtime built on ggml / llama.cpp. Three separate license regimes apply
to different parts of this project. Read all three.


1. ESM-C 300M model weights and their derivatives
--------------------------------------------------
The ESM-C 300M Open Model is licensed by EvolutionaryScale, PBC under the
EvolutionaryScale Cambrian Open License Agreement (a permissive license that
allows academic and commercial use). The GGUF weight files produced or
distributed by this project — including every `*.gguf` conversion and any
quantization of it — are Derivative Works of that model and are governed by the
same agreement:

    EvolutionaryScale Cambrian Open License Agreement
    https://www.evolutionaryscale.ai/policies/cambrian-open-license-agreement

Use of the model is additionally subject to the EvolutionaryScale Acceptable Use
Policy:

    https://www.evolutionaryscale.ai/policies/acceptable-use-policy

Per Section 4 (Redistribution) of that Agreement, anyone who redistributes the
model or a Derivative Work must, among other conditions:

  * give recipients a copy of the Agreement (linked above);
  * cause any modified files to carry prominent notices stating they changed
    them (the GGUF files here are format conversions of the original
    checkpoint — see NOTICE);
  * prominently display "Built with ESM" wherever the model is made available;
  * retain all copyright, patent, trademark, and attribution notices; and
  * include the attribution text from the NOTICE file in this repository.

The canonical agreement linked above is the controlling text; the summary above
is for convenience only.


2. esmc.cpp original source code (this repository's own code)
-------------------------------------------------------------
The original code written for this project is released under the MIT License
(reproduced below). This covers the C/C++ runtime under `src/` and `examples/`,
the Python converter / quantizer / benchmark harnesses under `tools/`, `tests/`,
and `benchmarks/`, and the project documentation. This runtime is separable from
and merely binds by name to the model interfaces, so it is not itself a
Derivative Work of the model; however, the MIT grant below does NOT relax any
obligation that the Cambrian Open License Agreement (Section 1) places on the
ESM-C model weights or their GGUF derivatives.


3. ggml / llama.cpp (the `ggml/` submodule)
-------------------------------------------
The `ggml/` directory is the upstream llama.cpp project, used as a git submodule,
and remains under its own MIT License. See `ggml/LICENSE`.


